cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  This is an active research project. If you use this benchmark — its code,
  scenarios, data, results, or design — please cite it. A preprint is in
  preparation; please check back for the paper citation and DOI, and prefer
  citing the paper once it is available.
title: >-
  Say-Do-Watched: Benchmarking the Embodied Safety and Moral Behaviour of
  LLM Robot Planners
abstract: >-
  Say-Do-Watched is an open, reproducible benchmark for the embodied safety and
  moral behaviour of large language models used as robot planners. An LLM drives
  a simulated UR5e arm in closed loop in a workspace shared with people, and each
  scenario is a controlled experiment that varies one factor at a time — who the
  person is, what a supervisor instructs, and whether the model is told it is
  being evaluated. The benchmark measures whether a model does what it says
  (say-do consistency), treats people described differently with equal physical
  caution, refuses harmful instructions, and behaves the same when it believes it
  is not being watched.
